WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.769
This video is about retrieval, usually called RAG, retrieval-augmented generation.

2
00:00:03.819 --> 00:00:10.262
Your agent will need facts that are not in the model: your team's documents, policies, product data.

3
00:00:10.312 --> 00:00:13.317
Retrieval is how they get into the context.

4
00:00:13.367 --> 00:00:20.574
It is also where many agents quietly go wrong, so the second half of the video is about failure.

5
00:00:21.324 --> 00:00:22.645
Here is the pipeline.

6
00:00:22.695 --> 00:00:33.960
Before anyone asks a question, you take the documents, split them into chunks, turn each chunk into an embedding, a list of numbers that captures its meaning, and store those in an index.

7
00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:42.531
At question time, you embed the question the same way, find the k chunks whose embeddings are closest, and put those chunks into the context.

8
00:00:42.581 --> 00:00:46.989
The model answers from them, ideally citing which chunk each claim came from.

9
00:00:47.039 --> 00:00:48.360
Four places to fail.

10
00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:53.160
Chunking: the answer was split across two chunks and neither one makes sense alone.

11
00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:57.618
Retrieval: the right chunk exists, but it was not in the top k.

12
00:00:57.668 --> 00:01:03.103
Reading: the right chunk was in the context, and the model ignored it or misread it.

13
00:01:03.153 --> 00:01:06.532
Refusing: nothing relevant was retrieved, and the model answered anyway.

14
00:01:07.282 --> 00:01:12.613
The most useful habit is to measure retrieval on its own, before you look at the answers.

15
00:01:12.663 --> 00:01:13.563
Write test questions.

16
00:01:13.613 --> 00:01:16.728
For each one, mark which chunks actually contain the answer.

17
00:01:16.778 --> 00:01:24.008
Then compute recall at k: the share of questions for which at least one of those chunks is in the top k results.

18
00:01:24.058 --> 00:01:25.907
Ten questions will get you started.

19
00:01:25.957 --> 00:01:26.857
Fifty is better.

20
00:01:26.907 --> 00:01:32.554
If recall at k is 60 percent, no prompt will make the answers more than 60 percent right.

21
00:01:32.604 --> 00:01:34.453
Only then look at the answers.

22
00:01:34.503 --> 00:01:36.669
Did the model use the retrieved text?

23
00:01:36.719 --> 00:01:41.100
Does every claim have a citation, and does the cited chunk actually support it?

24
00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:45.531
And when nothing relevant came back, did the model say it could not answer?

25
00:01:45.581 --> 00:01:49.013
That last behavior is the one you have to design for.

26
00:01:49.063 --> 00:01:50.912
Models do not refuse by default.

27
00:01:51.662 --> 00:01:53.395
Some fixes work almost everywhere.

28
00:01:53.445 --> 00:02:02.668
Chunk by the document's structure rather than by a fixed number of characters, so a section, a table, or a question and its answer stay together.

29
00:02:02.718 --> 00:02:08.374
Add metadata and filter on it, so a question about one product searches that product's documents.

30
00:02:08.424 --> 00:02:15.507
Combine keyword search with embedding search; product codes, names and error numbers are keywords, and embeddings are bad at them.

31
00:02:15.557 --> 00:02:21.926
Rerank a larger candidate set, say the top twenty, with a second model that picks the top five.

32
00:02:21.976 --> 00:02:30.129
And keep the source and the location with every chunk, so that a citation can point to a page a person can open.

33
00:02:30.879 --> 00:02:31.887
One more distinction.

34
00:02:31.937 --> 00:02:35.415
In a chatbot, retrieval happens once, before the model speaks.

35
00:02:35.465 --> 00:02:37.884
In an agent, search is a tool.

36
00:02:37.934 --> 00:02:45.997
The model decides when to call it, it can rewrite the query and search again, and it can stop when it has enough.

37
00:02:46.047 --> 00:02:53.405
That is more powerful and it is harder to evaluate, because the same question can produce different searches on different runs.

38
00:02:53.455 --> 00:03:00.812
It also means every search result lands in the context and costs tokens, which is why last video's trimming matters here.

39
00:03:01.562 --> 00:03:10.261
In the second part of lab three you build retrieval over your team's corpus with citations, write ten test questions, and report recall at k.

40
00:03:10.311 --> 00:03:13.760
Include one question whose answer is not in the corpus.

41
00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:17.610
The right answer to that question is: I cannot find that.

42
00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:22.859
If your agent answers it anyway, you have found your first failure to write up.

43
00:03:22.909 --> 00:03:30.558
Next video: memory, which is what the agent keeps between runs, and skills, which are instructions it loads when it needs them.
